      "source": [
        "## Indexing variables a dataset\n",
        "\n",
        "We can select a specific variables of a data frame using the syntaxis below."
      ],
      "id": "49fca6df"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "penguins_data['bill_length_mm']"
      ],
      "id": "c8037d92",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Here, we selected the variable `bill_length_mm` in the `penguins_data` dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "To index multiple variables of a data frame, we put the names of the variables in a list object. For example, we select `bill_length_mm`, `species`, and `island` as follows:"
      ],
      "id": "b2e54495"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "sub_penguins_data = penguins_data[ ['bill_length_mm',  'species', 'island'] ]\n",
        "sub_penguins_data.head()"
      ],
      "id": "fff8e714",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Indexing rows\n",
        "\n",
        "To index rows in a dataset, we use the argument `loc` from **pandas**. For example, we select the rows 3 to 6 of the `penguins_dataset` dataset:"
      ],
      "id": "96557812"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "rows_penguins_data = penguins_data.loc[2:5]\n",
        "rows_penguins_data"
      ],
      "id": "7322eeda",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "</br>"
      ],
      "id": "46926f02"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "rows_penguins_data"
      ],
      "id": "8c586b4c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Note that the index 2 and 5 refer to observations 3 and 7, respectively, in the dataset. This is because the first index in Python is 0.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Indexing rows and columns\n",
        "\n",
        "Using `loc`, we can also retrieve a subset from the dataset by selecting specific columns and rows."
      ],
      "id": "fdcf36a1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "sub_rows_pdata = penguins_data.loc[2:5, ['bill_length_mm',  'species', 'island'] ]\n",
        "sub_rows_pdata"
      ],
      "id": "56f6c422",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Mini-activity (*solo* mode)\n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "1.  Open the following <a href=\"https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1xLoaOMharQmmasNApjm38jZcw-gtcxvm?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Google Colab</a> link: <https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1xLoaOMharQmmasNApjm38jZcw-gtcxvm?usp=sharing>\n",
        "\n",
        "2.  Copy the notebook to your drive.\n",
        "\n",
        "3.  Answer the questions.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Data manipulation with pandas\n",
        "\n",
        "## Chaining operations with pandas\n",
        "\n",
        "One of the most important techniques in **pandas** is [**chaining**]{style=\"color:brown;\"}, which allows for cleaner and more readable data manipulation.\n",
        "\n",
        "The general structure of chaining looks like this:\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/chaining_pandas.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Key pandas methods\n",
        "\n",
        "**pandas** provides methods or functions to solve common data manipulation tasks:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: incremental\n",
        "- `.filter()` selects specific columns or rows.\n",
        "\n",
        "- `.query()` filters observations based on conditions.\n",
        "\n",
        "- `.assign()` adds new variables that are functions of existing variables.\n",
        "\n",
        "- `.sort_values()` changes the order of rows.\n",
        "\n",
        "- `.agg()` reduces multiple values to a single numerical summary.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/pandas_manipulation.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "To practice, we will use the dataset `penguins_data`.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's load the dataset and the **pandas** library."
      ],
      "id": "46b32f2c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "# Load the Excel file into a pandas DataFrame.\n",
        "penguins_data = pd.read_excel(\"penguins.xlsx\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Preview the dataset.\n",
        "penguins_data.head(4)"
      ],
      "id": "dba475a4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Selecting columns with `.filter()`\n",
        "\n",
        "Select the columns `species`, `body_mass_g` and `sex`."
      ],
      "id": "f3fb1a24"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .filter([\"species\", \"body_mass_g\", \"sex\"], axis = 1)\n",
        ").head()"
      ],
      "id": "4909997d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "The `axis` argument tells `.filter()` whether to select rows (`0`) or columns (`1`) from the dataframe."
      ],
      "id": "ff07dda5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .filter([\"species\", \"body_mass_g\", \"sex\"], axis = 1)\n",
        ").head()"
      ],
      "id": "e760ae2d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "> The `.head()` command allows us to print the first six rows of the newly produced dataframe. We **must** remove it to have the entire new dataframe.\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "We can also use `.filter()` to select rows too. To this end, we set `axis = 1`. We can select specific rows, such as 0 and 10."
      ],
      "id": "04185ee2"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .filter([0, 10], axis = 0)\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "e0012ca3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Or, we can select a set of rows using the function `range()`. For example, let's select the first 5 rows."
      ],
      "id": "31945030"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .filter(range(5), axis = 0)\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "6412c499",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Filtering rows with `.query()`\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "An alternative way of selecting rows is `.query()`. Compared to `.filter()`, `.query()` allows us to filter the data using statements or *queries* involving the variables.\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, let's filter the data for the species \"Gentoo.\""
      ],
      "id": "8febf839"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .query(\"species == 'Gentoo'\")\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "1ebeada7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "</br>"
      ],
      "id": "69ce73bf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .query(\"species == 'Gentoo'\")\n",
        ").head()"
      ],
      "id": "90abf2c5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "We can also filter the data to get penguins with a body mass greater than 5000g."
      ],
      "id": "b6f970bb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .query(\"body_mass_g > 5000\")\n",
        ").head()"
      ],
      "id": "88f18313",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "We can even **combine** `.filter()` and `.query()`. For example, let's select the columns `species`, `body_mass_g` and `sex`, then filter the data for the \"Gentoo\" species."
      ],
      "id": "aa683b71"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .filter([\"species\", \"body_mass_g\", \"sex\"], axis = 1)\n",
        "  .query(\"species == 'Gentoo'\")\n",
        ").head(4)"
      ],
      "id": "1c105418",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Create new columns with `.assign()`\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "With `.assign()`, we can create new columns (variables) that are functions of existing ones. This function uses a special Python keyword called `lambda`. Technically, this keyword defines an *anonymous* function.\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, we create a new variable `LDRatio` equaling the ratio of `bill_length_mm` and `bill_depth_mm`."
      ],
      "id": "1544846b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .assign(LDRatio = lambda df: df[\"bill_length_mm\"] / df[\"bill_depth_mm\"])\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "68631777",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "In this code, the `df` after `lambda` indicates that the dataframe (`penguins_data`) will be referred to as `df` inside the function. The colon `:` sets the start of the function."
      ],
      "id": "38d3bfcb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: false\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .assign(LDRatio = lambda df: df[\"bill_length_mm\"] / df[\"bill_depth_mm\"])\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "ce490dc5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The code appends the new variable to the end of the resulting dataframe.\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "We can see the new variable using `.filter()`."
      ],
      "id": "4628c90c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| output: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .assign(LDRatio = lambda df: df[\"bill_length_mm\"] / df[\"bill_depth_mm\"])\n",
        "  .filter([\"bill_length_mm\", \"bill_depth_mm\", \"LDRatio\"], axis = 1)\n",
        ").head()"
      ],
      "id": "49450454",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Sorting with `.sort_values()`\n",
        "\n",
        "We can sort the data based on a column like `bill_length_mm`."
      ],
      "id": "6bf23d62"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .sort_values(\"bill_length_mm\")\n",
        ").head(4)"
      ],
      "id": "77aa1323",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "To sort in descending order, use `ascending=False` inside `sort_values()`."
      ],
      "id": "e8aded83"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .sort_values(\"bill_length_mm\", ascending=False)\n",
        ").head()"
      ],
      "id": "532bc242",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Summarizing with `.agg()`\n",
        "\n",
        "We can calculate summary statistics of the columns `bill_length_mm`, `bill_depth_mm`, and `body_mass_g`."
      ],
      "id": "d01ab37c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "(penguins_data\n",
        "  .filter([\"bill_length_mm\", \"bill_depth_mm\", \"body_mass_g\"], axis = 1)\n",
        "  .agg([\"mean\"])\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "467beb92",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "> By default, `agg()` ignores missing values.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Saving results in new objects\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "After performing operations on our data, we can save the modified dataset as a new object."
      ],
      "id": "c10ced72"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: true\n",
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "mean_penguins_data = (penguins_data\n",
        "  .filter([\"bill_length_mm\", \"bill_depth_mm\", \"body_mass_g\"], axis = 1)\n",
        "  .agg([\"mean\"])\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "mean_penguins_data"
      ],
      "id": "27a27793",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
